+### [Hierarchical 3D Sampling](8510-hierarchical-3d-sampling.md)
+Hierarchical 3D Sampling generates a sequence of points within a unit cube such that any prefix of the sequence $\{P_0, \dots, P_{n-1}\}$ maintains uniform density and a minimum inter-point distance. This property allows the method to serve as a density control mechanism for volume rendering, enabling adaptive sampling based on opacity or managing the level of detail (LOD) by selecting a subset of pre-computed points. Unlike its siblings, which often rely on probabilistic distributions or neural networks, this method provides deterministic, hierarchical point sets where the "priority" of a point is defined by its index in the generation sequence.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \log M)$ per ray, where $N$ is the number of fine samples and $M$ is the number of coarse bins, dominated by the `searchsorted` operation [S4, S7, unofficial]. The PDF/CDF computation is $O(M)$.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(M)$ per ray to store the CDF and intermediate tensors [S7, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** In NeRF, using 64 coarse and 128 fine samples with hierarchical sampling significantly reduces PSNR degradation compared to uniform sampling with the same total sample count, effectively doubling the quality for the same computational budget [S3, S4, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Concentrates samples in high-density regions, improving reconstruction quality of thin structures and surfaces [S1, S3, unofficial]. Differentiable, allowing gradient flow to the coarse network [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires a coarse pass first, adding latency [S4, unofficial]. Performance depends on the quality of the coarse weights; poor coarse estimates lead to poor fine sampling [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** When computational resources are limited but high-quality reconstruction of complex geometry is needed [S3, unofficial].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When real-time performance is critical and a single pass is required, or when the scene is uniformly dense [G].
+
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N_{coarse} + N_{fine})$ per ray. The dominant operations are the cumulative sum and searchsorted, which are linear in the number of coarse bins and fine samples [S1, S12, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N_{coarse} + N_{fine})$ per ray to store the CDF and intermediate tensors [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** In NeRF implementations, using 64 coarse samples and 128 fine samples via hierarchical sampling significantly improves PSNR compared to uniform sampling with the same total sample count, particularly for scenes with thin structures or high-frequency details [S4, S12, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:**
+    *   Reduces variance in regions of high density/opacity.
+    *   Simple to implement and differentiable.
+    *   Works well with MLP-based radiance fields where density is explicitly modeled [S1, S12, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:**
+    *   Requires a coarse pass to generate weights, doubling the computational cost of the forward pass if not optimized [S1, unofficial].
+    *   Can still waste samples in empty space if the coarse density estimate is inaccurate [S1, unofficial].
+    *   Not suitable for skipping empty space entirely (unlike [RangeFineSampler](3141-rangefinesampler.md)) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** When rendering scenes with varying density where uniform sampling is inefficient, and a coarse estimate of importance is available. Common in NeRF and similar implicit neural representations [S1, S12, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use:** When memory is extremely constrained (due to storing CDF), or when the scene is mostly empty and skipping is preferred [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Hierarchical Sampling was introduced in the seminal NeRF paper "NeRF: Representing Scenes as Neural Radiance Fields for View Synthesis" by Mildenhall et al. (2020) [S2, S9, unofficial]. It was developed to address the inefficiency of uniform sampling in neural radiance fields, where most samples fall in empty space. The method has since become a standard component in many NeRF variants and volume rendering pipelines [S1, S3, S4, unofficial]. It is widely used in academic research and open-source implementations such as the official NeRF repository and various "NeRF from Scratch" tutorials [S2, S3, S4, unofficial].
+
+Hierarchical Sampling was introduced in the seminal NeRF paper "NeRF: Representing Scenes as Neural Radiance Fields for View Synthesis" by Mildenhall et al. (2020) [S2, S12, unofficial]. It was designed to address the inefficiency of uniform sampling in volume rendering, where many samples fall in empty space. By using the density predicted by a coarse network to guide the sampling of a fine network, NeRF achieved high-quality novel view synthesis with manageable computational cost [S2, S12, unofficial]. The method has since become a standard component in many neural rendering pipelines, including Instant NGP and various other NeRF variants [S3, S4, unofficial].
 
